course: dictionary
lesson: 8355
title: Rot and its many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is right. Rot can be a verb (something rots) or a noun (the rot itself). «Fish should get only as much food as they eat in a couple of minutes, because leftovers rot and poison the water.» Here rot means to go bad and break down, because leftover food spoils.
explain: Rot can also mean to make something decay. «Sugar rots your teeth» means sugar makes teeth go bad. Read the words around rot: is something going bad by itself, is something causing it, or is it a part that has already gone bad?
explain: Sometimes rot means nonsense. «Don't talk rot!» is not about food or wood at all. The clue is the talking: the person is saying silly things that are not true or sensible. Always swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Rot means to go soft, bad and break down slowly, often because of bacteria and fungi.
fact: Rotting is the -ing form of rot: «the rotting meat» means meat that has gone bad.
fact: As a noun, rot is the decay itself, or a part that has gone bad.
fact: Decomposers such as bacteria, fungi and worms make dead plants and animals rot, putting nutrients back into the soil. This is how compost works.
fact: Food rots faster when it is warm, so keep it in the fridge. Rot can also mean nonsense, but this use is informal and mostly old British.
exercise: «Leftovers rot and poison the water.» What does rot mean here? | To sing loudly | *To go bad and break down | To be very hungry | To shine brightly
exercise: «Sugar rots your teeth.» What does rots mean here? | Makes them shine white | Makes them warm and dry | Makes them grow longer | *Makes them go bad and break down
exercise: «A flower that stinks of rotting meat.» What does rotting mean? | Dancing in the wind | *Going bad and smelling | Very hungry | Growing bright and colourful
test: «Don't talk rot!» What does rot mean? | Sweet treats | A tall tree trunk | *Nonsense | Dirt in the soil
test: «Cut away the rot from the old fence post.» What is the rot here? | *The part that has gone bad | A bright, sunny patch | A kind of green paint | A happy song
exam: «Dead leaves rot into the ground and feed the soil.» What does rot mean here? | Glow in the dark | Float on the water | *Go bad and break down | Freeze solid
